+  <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+    <article class="journal-inner">
+      <img class="journal-image"
+        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1447933601403-0c6688de566e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80"
+        alt="Freshly roasted coffee beans" loading="lazy" width="1350" height="260">
+      <div class="journal-body">
+        <div class="journal-header">
+          <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+          <p class="journal-meta">From the roastery floor &middot; Notes on how we source our beans</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+          <p>Every bag of coffee that leaves our roastery starts with a relationship, not a catalog. Long before a single
+            bean hits our drum roaster, our sourcing team has spent months talking with growers, cooperatives, and
+            importers about the harvest ahead. We travel to origin at least twice a year&mdash;to the highlands of
+            Huila in Colombia, the washing stations of Yirgacheffe in Ethiopia, and the volcanic slopes of Antigua in
+            Guatemala&mdash;because there is no substitute for standing on a farm and seeing how the coffee is grown,
+            picked, and processed.</p>
+
+          <p>We buy almost exclusively through direct trade. That means we negotiate prices with producers face to face,
+            and we commit to paying well above the commodity market rate&mdash;typically 40 to 80 percent more than the
+            Fair Trade minimum. Those premiums are not charity; they are an investment in quality. When farmers are paid
+            fairly, they can afford to pick only ripe cherries, replant aging trees, and experiment with new processing
+            methods. The result is a better cup for you and a more stable livelihood for them.</p>
+
+          <p>Quality control begins at origin. Before we commit to a lot, we cup dozens of pre-shipment samples side by
+            side, scoring each on aroma, acidity, body, sweetness, and finish. Only coffees that score 85 points or higher
+            on the Specialty Coffee Association scale make it onto our menu. Once a lot is approved, we track it all the
+            way to our door, and we cup it again on arrival to make sure nothing changed in transit. Green coffee is a
+            living agricultural product, and humidity, heat, and time can all dull its character.</p>
+
+          <p>Sustainability is woven into every decision. We prioritize farms that grow under shade canopy, which
+            protects biodiversity and keeps soil healthy, and we support partners transitioning to organic practices
+            even before they can afford formal certification. Many of our producers have invested in water-saving
+            eco-pulpers and composting programs that turn coffee pulp back into fertilizer. We ship green coffee in
+            reusable jute and GrainPro bags, and we offset the carbon footprint of every container we import.</p>
+
+          <p>Finally, we roast in small batches of no more than twelve kilograms, adjusting each profile to bring out
+            what makes a particular coffee special&mdash;the jasmine and bergamot of a washed Ethiopian, the chocolate
+            and red apple of a Colombian, the brown sugar depth of a Guatemalan. We roast four days a week so that the
+            beans in your cup are never more than a week past their roast date. Sourcing well is slow, careful work, but
+            we believe you can taste the difference in every sip.</p>
